  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nBroad ridging is still expected across the southwest this week. \nThe ridge is expected to strengthen and expand westward Thursday \nand Friday settling over the Arizona/New Mexico border for \nSaturday into early next week. High temperatures will start to \nbecome above average on Wednesday, peaking at 5 to 10 degrees \nabove average Thursday through the weekend. In addition to above \naverage high temperatures, low temperatures will be running 5 to \n10 degrees above average, locally up to 15 degrees above average \nin the mountains. The combination of elevated high and low \ntemperatures will limit the ability to recover from heat overnight\nand allow for conditions to quickly warm once the sun rises. \nWidespread moderate to locally major HeatRisk is expected in \ninland Orange County, San Diego Valleys, Inland Empire, and area \nmountains. Major HeatRisk is expected in the deserts, with locally\nextreme HeatRisk in Coachella Valley and San Diego deserts. Major\nto extreme HeatRisk indicates that anyone may be negatively \nimpacted by the heat if the proper precautions are not taken. \n\nWhile there will be a minor uptick in monsoonal moisture in the \nmountains and deserts Thursday and Friday, chances of showers and \nthunderstorms stay low. Shower chances are mostly 15 to 20 percent, \nwith only a 5 percent chance of thunderstorms developing. Dry \nconditions are expected over the weekend into early next week.",
